# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Joshua'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'Crossing the Jordan, the Conquest, and the Land Divided by Lot'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Joshua follows Israel after the death of Moses, when the LORD says, “Moses my servant is dead; now therefore arise, go over this Jordan” (Josh. 1:2). It records the crossing of the Jordan, the fall of Jericho, the battles of the conquest, the division of the land among the tribes, the cities of refuge, and Joshua’s last words and the covenant renewed at Shechem (Josh. 3:17; 6:20; 11:23; 20:2; 24:15).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel is God’s covenant nation brought into the land sworn to its fathers and given to it by tribe (Josh. 1:6; 11:23).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Joshua', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Preparing to Enter (Chs. 1–5)'), ('p', 'The LORD commissions Joshua, Rahab hides the spies, Israel crosses the Jordan on dry ground, and the captain of the host of the LORD appears (Josh. 1:2, 9; 2:11; 3:17; 5:14).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — The Conquest (Chs. 6–12)'), ('p', 'Jericho falls, Israel is judged and restored at Ai, and the land is taken in the south and the north (Josh. 6:20; 10:14; 11:23).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Land Divided (Chs. 13–21)'), ('p', 'The land that remains is named, the inheritance is divided by tribe, and cities of refuge are appointed (Josh. 13:1; 14:12; 20:2; 21:45).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Joshua’s Farewell (Chs. 22–24)'), ('p', 'Joshua charges Israel to keep the law of Moses, rehearses what the LORD has done, and the people renew their covenant (Josh. 23:6, 14; 24:2, 13, 15).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Joshua', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Land Given</b> — “Every place that the sole of your foot shall tread upon, that have I given unto you, as I said unto Moses” (Josh. 1:3).', '<b>The Book of the Law</b> — “This book of the law shall not depart out of thy mouth” (Josh. 1:8).', '<b>The LORD Fights for Israel</b> — “There was no day like that before it or after it, that the LORD hearkened unto the voice of a man: for the LORD fought for Israel” (Josh. 10:14).', '<b>Rest from War</b> — “Joshua gave it for an inheritance unto Israel according to their divisions by their tribes. And the land rested from war” (Josh. 11:23).', '<b>Every Promise Kept</b> — “There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel; all came to pass” (Josh. 21:45).', '<b>Choose Whom to Serve</b> — “Choose you this day whom ye will serve… but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD” (Josh. 24:15).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Joshua', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Joshua points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>Joshua the Deliverer</b> — Joshua leads Israel into the land and divides the inheritance (Josh. 11:23). His name is the Old Testament form of the name Jesus, and the book reads as Israel’s own deliverance within the Prophecy Program.', '<b>The Captain of the Host</b> — Joshua meets one who says, “as captain of the host of the LORD am I now come,” and Joshua worships (Josh. 5:14). The verse is reported as written.', '<b>The Cities of Refuge</b> — The LORD tells Joshua to appoint cities of refuge (Josh. 20:2), part of Israel’s covenant provision in the land.', '<b>The Promise Kept</b> — “Not one thing hath failed” of the good things the LORD spoke to Israel (Josh. 23:14).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Joshua', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Joshua speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Joshua operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Joshua in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Joshua reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['crossing into the land sworn to its fathers (Josh. 1:6; 3:17)', 'conquest and rest from war (Josh. 11:23)', 'inheritance divided by tribe (Josh. 13:1; 14:12)', 'cities of refuge (Josh. 20:2)', 'covenant renewed at Shechem (Josh. 24:15)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Joshua that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Joshua contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Commissioned for the Land — “Moses my servant is dead; now therefore arise, go over this Jordan.” (Josh. 1:2).', 'The Land Sworn — “unto this people shalt thou divide for an inheritance the land, which I sware unto their fathers to give them.” (Josh. 1:6).', 'The Book of the Law — “This book of the law shall not depart out of thy mouth.” (Josh. 1:8).', 'The Jordan Crossed — “all the Israelites passed over on dry ground.” (Josh. 3:17).', 'Jericho Falls — “the wall fell down flat, so that the people went up into the city.” (Josh. 6:20).', 'The LORD Fought for Israel — “the LORD fought for Israel.” (Josh. 10:14).', 'The Land Divided — “Joshua gave it for an inheritance unto Israel according to their divisions by their tribes.” (Josh. 11:23).', 'Cities of Refuge — “Appoint out for you cities of refuge.” (Josh. 20:2).', 'Every Word Fulfilled — “There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel.” (Josh. 21:45).', 'The Law of Moses Kept — “to keep and to do all that is written in the book of the law of Moses.” (Josh. 23:6).', 'The Covenant Renewed — “choose you this day whom ye will serve.” (Josh. 24:15).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Joshua Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Joshua and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Joshua teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Joshua', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Commissioned for the Land', '“Moses my servant is dead; now therefore arise, go over this Jordan.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 1:2'], ['The Land Sworn', '“unto this people shalt thou divide for an inheritance the land, which I sware unto their fathers to give them.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 1:6'], ['The Book of the Law', '“This book of the law shall not depart out of thy mouth.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 1:8'], ['The Jordan Crossed', '“all the Israelites passed over on dry ground.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 3:17'], ['Jericho Falls', '“the wall fell down flat, so that the people went up into the city.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 6:20'], ['The LORD Fought for Israel', '“the LORD fought for Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 10:14'], ['The Land Divided', '“Joshua gave it for an inheritance unto Israel according to their divisions by their tribes.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 11:23'], ['Cities of Refuge', '“Appoint out for you cities of refuge.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 20:2'], ['Every Word Fulfilled', '“There failed not ought of any good thing which the LORD had spoken unto the house of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 21:45'], ['The Law of Moses Kept', '“to keep and to do all that is written in the book of the law of Moses.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 23:6'], ['The Covenant Renewed', '“choose you this day whom ye will serve.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Josh. 24:15']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Joshua that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Joshua teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['The LORD Is With You', '“the LORD thy God is with thee whithersoever thou goest.”', 'Josh. 1:9'], ['The LORD Is God', '“the LORD your God, he is God in heaven above, and in earth beneath.”', 'Josh. 2:11'], ['Faithful to His Word', '“not one thing hath failed of all the good things which the LORD your God spake.”', 'Josh. 23:14']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Joshua without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Joshua in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Be strong and courageous</b> — The LORD is with you (Josh. 1:9).', '<b>Know who God is</b> — He is God in heaven above and earth beneath (Josh. 2:11).', '<b>Count on His faithfulness</b> — Not one thing hath failed (Josh. 23:14).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Joshua', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Joshua in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Preparing to Enter (Chs. 1–5) — The commission and the crossing. <i>(Josh. 1:2, 9; 3:17)</i>', 'The Conquest (Chs. 6–12) — Jericho and the battles. <i>(Josh. 6:20; 10:14; 11:23)</i>', 'The Land Divided (Chs. 13–21) — Inheritance and refuge. <i>(Josh. 14:12; 20:2; 21:45)</i>', 'Joshua’s Farewell (Chs. 22–24) — The covenant renewed. <i>(Josh. 23:6, 14; 24:15)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Joshua, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward courage, trust, and faithfulness without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Joshua simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Joshua stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
